{"_links":{"self":{"href":"/api/v2/datasets/doi%3A10.5061%2Fdryad.n2z34tmvv"},"stash:versions":{"href":"/api/v2/datasets/doi%3A10.5061%2Fdryad.n2z34tmvv/versions"},"stash:version":{"href":"/api/v2/versions/131027"},"stash:download":{"href":"/api/v2/datasets/doi%3A10.5061%2Fdryad.n2z34tmvv/download"},"curies":[{"name":"stash","href":"https://github.com/datadryad/dryad-app/blob/main/documentation/apis/link_relations.md#{rel}","templated":"true"}]},"identifier":"doi:10.5061/dryad.n2z34tmvv","id":60933,"storageSize":3015012737,"relatedPublicationISSN":"2056-2799","title":"The first Silurian trilobite with three-dimensionally preserved soft parts reveals novel appendage morphology","authors":[{"firstName":"Mark","lastName":"Sutton","email":"m.sutton@ic.ac.uk","affiliation":"Imperial College London","affiliationROR":"https://ror.org/041kmwe10","affiliations":[{"organization":"Imperial College London","identifier":"https://ror.org/041kmwe10"}],"orcid":"0000-0002-7137-7572"},{"firstName":"Derek","lastName":"Siveter","email":"derek.siveter@oum.ox.ac.uk","affiliation":"University of Oxford","affiliationROR":"https://ror.org/052gg0110","affiliations":[{"organization":"University of Oxford","identifier":"https://ror.org/052gg0110"}],"orcid":"0000-0002-5305-2192"},{"firstName":"Richard","lastName":"Fortey","email":"","affiliation":"Natural History Museum","affiliationROR":"https://ror.org/039zvsn29","affiliations":[{"organization":"Natural History Museum","identifier":"https://ror.org/039zvsn29"}]},{"firstName":"Derek","lastName":"Briggs","email":"","affiliation":"Yale University","affiliationROR":"https://ror.org/03v76x132","affiliations":[{"organization":"Yale University","identifier":"https://ror.org/03v76x132"}]},{"firstName":"David","lastName":"Siveter","email":"","affiliation":"University of Leicester","affiliationROR":"https://ror.org/04h699437","affiliations":[{"organization":"University of Leicester","identifier":"https://ror.org/04h699437"}]}],"abstract":"\u003cp\u003eThe first Silurian trilobite known with soft parts preserved, a \u003ci\u003eDalmanites\u003c/i\u003e species, is described from the Herefordshire Lagerstätte. Biramous appendages and much of the alimentary system are evident.  High-fidelity three-dimensional preservation reveals a novel, double arrangement of the exopod filaments, interconnected by a presumed membranous sheet. This morphology explains a misinterpretation of the exopod as supporting spiral structures, originally reported nearly 150 years ago. The new exopod morphology is considered primarily respiratory in function and comparison with other trilobite limbs suggests that it may be unique to Phacopida.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","funders":[{"organization":"Natural Environment Research Council","identifierType":"ror","identifier":"https://ror.org/02b5d8509","awardNumber":"NE/F018037/1"},{"organization":"Leverhulme Trust","identifierType":"ror","identifier":"https://ror.org/012mzw131","awardNumber":"EM-2014-068"}],"keywords":["Trilobita","appendages","soft-part morphology","Herefordshire Lagerstätte"],"fieldOfScience":"Earth and related environmental sciences","methods":"\u003cp\u003eTomographic data collected through physical-optical tomography at 20-micron intervals, specimen is Oxford University Museum of Natural History OUMNH C.29611. See paper associated with this data for details\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\r\n\u003cp\u003e3D models generated from tomographic data using the SPIERS suite - see https://spiers-software.org/ - and see paper associated with this data for details.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","usageNotes":"\u003cp style=\"padding:0px;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"font-style:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"font-weight:400;\"\u003e\u003cspan style=\"letter-spacing:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"white-space:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThis dataset includes 6 zip files.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\r\n\u003cp style=\"padding:0px;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"font-style:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"font-weight:400;\"\u003e\u003cspan style=\"letter-spacing:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"white-space:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAppendageDetail_VAXML.zip contains a detailed VAXML 3D model for appendage 5.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\r\n\u003cp style=\"padding:0px;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"font-style:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"font-weight:400;\"\u003e\u003cspan style=\"letter-spacing:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"white-space:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eDalmanites_sp_VAXML.zip contains a VAXML 3D model for the entire specimen, OUMNH C.29611\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\r\n\u003cp style=\"padding:0px;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"font-style:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"font-weight:400;\"\u003e\u003cspan style=\"letter-spacing:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan style=\"white-space:normal;\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eSection1-Cephalon.zip, Section2-AnteriorThorax.zip, Section3-PosteriorThorax.zip and Section4-Thorax-Pygidium.zip contain between them all tomograms (‘slice images’) for the specimen OUMNH C.29611. Tomograms are registered but otherwise unprocessed.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\r\n\r\n\u003cp\u003e3D models are in VAXML/STL format. VAXML is an open metadata format for a combininng a series of 3D geometry files stored in the readily viewable STL format.  See https://spiers-software.org/ for freely available viewing software for VAXML datasets, and for further information.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n","relatedWorks":[{"relationship":"primary_article","identifierType":"DOI","identifier":"https://doi.org/10.1002/spp2.1401"}],"versionNumber":6,"versionStatus":"submitted","curationStatus":"Published","versionChanges":"metadata_changed","publicationDate":"2021-07-21","lastModificationDate":"2021-07-22","visibility":"public","sharingLink":"http://datadryad.org/dataset/doi:10.5061/dryad.n2z34tmvv","changedFields":["journal","title","related_identifiers",[{"index":0,"id":"https://doi.org/10.1002/spp2.1401","type":"primary_article","relation":"iscitedby"}]],"license":"https://spdx.org/licenses/CC0-1.0.html","metrics":{"views":175,"downloads":19,"citations":1}}